Rajeev, you know, why Java?

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:48.560
Why is Java important to GCP?

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.170
What's the story there?

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.770
RAJEEV DAYAL: Well, Java--

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.460
you know, it's an
important language.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.490
It's been, I guess, really
since like the mid '90s.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.480
And then, since then it's
just become pretty much

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.080
a staple language that's
used everywhere, honestly.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.060
It's used very
heavily in enterprise.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.420
I think the language index--
the TIOBE index-- still

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:16.550
shows Java as the most heavily
used language across the board

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:17.800
for all sorts of applications.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.910
So yeah, it's just a
hugely important language.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.800
I think one of the reasons
for that is because it's just

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.170
pretty versatile as well.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.730
It's one of those languages.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.390
Some people say like, oh, you
know which is more expressive,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:33.514
or it's too verbose.

00:08:33.514 --> 00:08:35.809
And some of those
things may be true.

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:38.299
But it's also shown
that you can build

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:41.270
like really large applications
and services with it.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.100
You can have like
really big code bases,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.830
and they're still maintainable.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:49.490
So it's really important that
we support Java developers

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.760
and such workloads on GCP,
especially as more enterprises

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.124
are moving into the Cloud.

00:08:54.124 --> 00:08:55.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.620
So I know that one
of the places where

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.070
we talk about Java all the time
on the cloud is App Engine.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:01.820
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yes.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
what other things

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.040
are there in Google
Cloud Platform

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.770
that are related to Java?

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:10.470
And what is the
best place to run

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:11.720
Java on Google Cloud Platform?

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:15.680
RAJEEV DAYAL: So I'll
take a stab at this,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.690
and then Ray can jump in.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:20.180
I think it really depends
on what you're doing, right?

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:26.180
So App Engine standard
actually is like a good place

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.540
to run Java if you really
care about very sort

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.790
of spiky traffic-- very
fast, sort of like scale up

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:33.230
and scale down.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:35.330
You want to have
reactiveness to that.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.090
And you're OK with sort of
being a little more constrained

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:39.800
in what you do, because
you want the auto

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:40.800
scaling to work for you.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.840
So that's a very good
environment for Java, actually,

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.060
because you don't have to
think about the scaling.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:48.830
If you're working
with containers,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.851
you know Kubernetes, and
then running it on GKE

00:09:51.851 --> 00:09:53.600
is obviously a great
environment for that.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.099
This is basically
hosted Kubernetes.

00:09:55.099 --> 00:09:57.380
So there you kind of
have more control.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.885
You're working with
containers and Kubernetes.

00:10:00.885 --> 00:10:03.260
Especially if you're sort of
breaking your application up

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.850
into microservices, you can
handle like pretty heavy

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:08.756
traffic and whatnot.

00:10:08.756 --> 00:10:10.380
You can kind of do
what you want there.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.940
And then of course you've got
Google Compute Engine, or GCE.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:14.990
And that's just a raw VM.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:16.679
So obviously, you
can do what you want.

00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:18.470
It's just that all the
set up is up to you.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.390
So there's lots of
different places

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:24.150
to run Java workloads on
Google's Cloud Platform.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.995
It really depends on
what you want to do.

00:10:25.995 --> 00:10:27.620
MARK MANDEL: And I
believe you can also

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:29.420
run Java on some of
our big data stuff

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.400
as well, like Dataflow
and some other tools.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:31.790
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:32.810
That's correct, actually.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:33.434
That's correct.

00:10:33.434 --> 00:10:38.620
Actually, the data flow
SDK is actually Java.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.772
And so, yeah, that's
also really popular.

00:10:41.772 --> 00:10:42.730
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:45.230
Ray, I know there are some other
places where Java is supported,

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.820
not just to be able
to just run your code,

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.080
but other things like that--

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.914
so like tooling and
SDKs and IDEs and stuff.

00:10:49.914 --> 00:10:51.830
Ray, you want to talk a
little bit about that?

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:52.910
RAY TSANG: Sure.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:57.050
Actually, Rajeev and another
engineer-- a team lead here,

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.200
who's not here today on
the podcast, but Patrick--

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.440
they actually developed
all the tunings for Java.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.330
So we have really
good tooling support

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.130
for if you are into
building tools.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:10.900
So for Maven and greater,
we have plugins for those.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:14.540
For the IDEs, we also
have IDE integrations

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.130
with Eclipse and InelliJ.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:20.840
So the idea, there, is for you
to be able to start a project

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:26.360
and develop your applications,
and be able to deploy into some

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:28.220
of those environments
that Rajeev just

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:31.510
mentioned, seamlessly,
directly from the IDE.

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:33.950
But also, if you need
to integrate with say

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:36.890
a CICD platform of some sort,
or manage your own deployment,

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:39.020
you can also do that
from the command line--

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.950
from your plugins as well
as from the build tools.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:44.960
RAJEEV DAYAL: And
yeah, just adding

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.210
on to that a little bit--

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:50.300
we've had, in the past, plugins
for Eclipse-- like the Google

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.360
plugin for Eclipse, which has
been there for a long time

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.620
and is being used to
support GWT and actually

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:59.030
App Engine for a long time.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:01.850
And we've invested
back in that, and we're

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.700
building new Eclipse tooling
that is purely Cloud focused,

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:05.450
actually.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:08.870
So we're putting a lot more
investment into Eclipse.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.874
And we'll expand that
functionality as well,

00:12:11.874 --> 00:12:14.540
to handle things like Dataflow--
to make it a lot easier to work

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:15.123
with Dataflow.

00:12:15.123 --> 00:12:18.372
There already is a plugin
for Eclipse for Dataflow.

00:12:18.372 --> 00:12:19.830
We'll integrate
those two together,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.039
so it will be in one package.

00:12:21.039 --> 00:12:22.579
MARK MANDEL: So I've
also been seeing

00:12:22.579 --> 00:12:24.836
a lot of activity on the
IntelliJ plug-in as well.

00:12:24.836 --> 00:12:26.710
Can you talk a little
bit about that as well?

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:27.501
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:12:27.501 --> 00:12:32.850
Actually, so that whole thing
started with Android Studio.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:35.210
So Android Studio
was released in--

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.330
I think it was like 2013.

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.090
And it is built on
the IntelliJ Platform,

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:41.990
as I think most people know now.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.740
And actually, we did
Cloud integration in there

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.180
like a while back, actually.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:51.250
And the idea back then was like
create an App Engine back end,

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.130
and use Cloud Endpoints
to wire that up

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.285
to your mobile application,
with the thought being

00:12:55.285 --> 00:12:57.410
that, OK, you just want a
simple back end and a way

00:12:57.410 --> 00:12:59.030
to talk to it with REST.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.490
Now we have other solutions,
like Firebase, which

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:02.684
are better suited to that.

00:13:02.684 --> 00:13:04.350
But that's kind of
what we started with.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.770
And then what we did was we
basically spun that back out

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:12.260
and improved it into an IntelliJ
plugin for community edition

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:14.090
and ultimate, to be
able to just like--

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.460
if you're just a Cloud
developer-- to be

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.366
able to work with App Engine.

00:13:18.366 --> 00:13:19.740
And that's going
to be our basis,

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.198
and we're going to
expand upon that

00:13:21.198 --> 00:13:23.766
and build more GCB
features into that plug-in.

00:13:23.766 --> 00:13:24.890
And, you know, it's timely.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:28.580
Because IntelliJ's popularity
has really taken off

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.870
in the past two or three years.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.070
So we really need to have
a good story in both IDEs,

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.544
because the community is
split across both of them.

00:13:36.544 --> 00:13:38.210
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I
use IntelliJ a lot.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.000
I don't actually
write in Java in it.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:40.940
I use it for everything else.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:41.270
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:42.060
I'd be like that too, right?

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.730
MARK MANDEL: But I'm a huge fan.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:46.490
It's pretty good for
almost every language

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:47.530
that it supports.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:48.330
They're very good.

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:48.830
Yeah.

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:49.746
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:49.746 --> 00:13:51.770
So, since we're talking
about other languages,

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:54.530
what about the other
languages that run on the JVM?

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.780
We've been talking
about mainly about Java.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.850
But what if I have
a Scala application?

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.590
Can I run Scala on--

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.010
I mean, of course I can run
it on Google Compute Engine.

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:04.810
But can I run it
also on App Engine?

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:06.810
RAY TSANG: Yeah, I
can take a stab at it.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.410
So, for example,
with App Engine Flex,

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.750
you can basically run
whatever you want.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:17.000
So it's not limited to just pure
Java or any certain languages

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:18.050
that this supports.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:21.500
But if you really want to
you, can definitely just

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.560
write it in whatever languages
you wish to write in, including

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.860
any of the other JVM
languages-- and just

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:31.640
being able to package it up
properly, or even just package

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.410
it up into a docker container.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:36.140
And as long as you're
listening to the right ports,

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:40.550
and responding to the right
health check endpoints,

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:43.820
we will be able to serve
those applications directly

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.040
in App Engine
Flexible as a pass.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.040
Now, of course if you
have the container,

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:52.340
you can also always just run
it in Google Container Engine,

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:55.590
if you need fine control
over your application.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.514
So you could do it either way.

00:14:58.514 --> 00:14:59.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:00.320
MARK MANDEL: What
about on standard?

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:01.030
Is it a but hacky?

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:01.613
I have to ask.

00:15:01.613 --> 00:15:02.681
I'm curious.

00:15:02.681 --> 00:15:04.156
RAY TSANG: Ha, ha, ha.

00:15:04.156 --> 00:15:06.030
MARK MANDEL: If I want
to take my Closure app

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:07.660
and run it on App Engine
Standard, can I do that?

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:08.784
RAY TSANG: Now, it depends.

00:15:08.784 --> 00:15:10.403
So does Closure app--

00:15:10.403 --> 00:15:12.741
can you actually package
it into a WAR file?

00:15:12.741 --> 00:15:14.490
Because you need to
be able to deploy that

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.890
into Tomcat or sort of a
Jetty container at the moment.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:19.590
So for example, you cannot--

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:21.120
at the moment--
run any of the JAR

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.910
files if you have a
self-executable JAR.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.190
But if you can
package up your app

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:29.540
in standard Jetty
container in a WAR file,

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.010
then you may be able to run it.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.409
You should give it a try, Mark.

00:15:34.409 --> 00:15:35.200
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:36.100
I bet you if you--

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:37.140
MARK MANDEL: I'll
take it for a spin.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:38.848
RAJEEV DAYAL: --if
you do enough hackery,

00:15:38.848 --> 00:15:41.071
there's probably some way
to get it going, right?

00:15:41.071 --> 00:15:41.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.710
I bet you there is some
way to get it going.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:44.540
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:46.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And could you share

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:50.010
if there's any actual
plans on doing more

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.120
about these languages?

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:57.050
Like, for instance, we recently
had someone new in our team--

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.410
Guillaume Laforge.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:02.040
He created Groovy.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.050
What about Groovy--
on App Engine Standard

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:05.490
I'm talking about?

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.970
RAJEEV DAYAL: So that's a
good question, actually.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:13.170
I think there isn't anything
directly in the pipeline

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.720
that I know about
to support that.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:18.572
But I do know
they're working to--

00:16:18.572 --> 00:16:21.030
this sounds kind of weird-- to
make the App Engine Standard

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.910
environment slightly
more flexible, you know,

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:27.060
so that you could
basically have less--

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.520
you're still running Java.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:32.080
You're kind of in a JVM, but
there's fewer restrictions.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:34.174
So in a world like
that, I could see

00:16:34.174 --> 00:16:35.340
more of that being possible.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.399
But I couldn't concretely
say, yet, honestly.

00:16:37.399 --> 00:16:38.315
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:38.315 --> 00:16:38.970
MARK MANDEL: That's fair.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:40.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I have a question.

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.980
As some of you may know, I
don't write Java that often.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:46.020
I'm more of a Go person.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:47.820
I know, it's shocking.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.507
RAJEEV DAYAL: That's OK.

00:16:49.507 --> 00:16:51.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
(LAUGHING) That's OK.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.714
One of the things
that I've heard about

00:16:52.714 --> 00:16:56.220
is that, when you create
microservices in Java,

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.045
it's not really a microservice
because the JVM is so big.

00:16:59.045 --> 00:17:02.520
Is that actually a problem?

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.260
I'm sincerely
asking the question.

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:07.859
For instance, if
you have on GKE,

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:09.839
is a container
going to take longer

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:13.190
to start because of
the JVM being large?

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:15.930
Or is there any of
those issues that people

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.640
that are writing Java
should take into account?

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.660
RAJEEV DAYAL: So as far as
microservice is concerned,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:24.089
the actual code that
you write in Java--

00:17:24.089 --> 00:17:25.950
given the recent
framework developments,

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.500
like Spring Boot and
many, many others--

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:32.490
you actually write very
few codes to achieve a lot,

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:36.990
because of all the common tasks
that these frameworks will be

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.960
able to take care of for you.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:42.150
And so the code that you
write is actually quite small.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.690
You can really focus
on the business problem

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:46.680
that you need to solve.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:48.600
In the end, the package--

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:52.770
the actual binary file that you
compile-- is also very small.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:55.890
So the JVM is really
there to support

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.036
the runtime environment.

00:17:57.036 --> 00:17:58.660
Now, with docker
containers, of course,

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.610
there's the concept of layering.

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:04.860
So you can layer that JVM
image underneath of your app,

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:07.980
so that you don't have to, say,
redeploy hundreds of megabytes

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:10.290
of JVM every single time.

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.080
So the actual app
that you deploy

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:16.590
could be as small as the code
that actually just changed.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.030
And that is a powerful
concept, because now

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:23.616
that will then give you a very
rapid deployment across all

00:18:23.616 --> 00:18:24.740
the services that you have.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:29.030
You don't have to really
manage which version of JVM

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:30.840
should be running,
because all of that

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:32.890
will be encapsulated in
one of the image layers

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:33.980
that you depend on.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:35.940
And the startup time
is actually quite fast.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.660
I mean, these apps can start
in seconds, to be honest.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.394
So that shouldn't
be much of an issue.

00:18:41.394 --> 00:18:42.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:43.060
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:44.250
Nice segue, I think.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.410
You mentioned Spring there.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:48.000
I know we've also talked about
Jetty in a couple of things.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.520
If I'm a Java developer, and
I'm going back to a while

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:53.046
when I used to live
a lot in the JVM,

00:18:53.046 --> 00:18:55.184
are there are particular
frameworks, or--

00:18:55.184 --> 00:18:56.850
as you were talking
about Jetty-- things

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:58.980
like basically
runtime environments,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.890
I guess, that are better
supported than others?

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.547
So if I'm coming to GCP, and
I'm going to write a Java app,

00:19:05.547 --> 00:19:07.380
should I be looking at
particular frameworks

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.036
or particular runtimes
to get my app running

00:19:10.036 --> 00:19:11.160
so I can run it on the web?

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:11.730
Does that make sense?

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.440
RAY TSANG: So at the
very basic level,

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.760
there's the GAE standards
with the servlet.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.580
So that's definitely
supported, right?

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:20.850
So even today, I mean, since
the App Engine standard,

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.715
GAE spec has been
supported in a way.

00:19:23.715 --> 00:19:24.840
So you can write a servlet.

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:26.550
You can serve your JSPs--

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.605
very standard Java stuff.

00:19:28.605 --> 00:19:33.180
In Flex, you can run even
more, basically removing

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.780
some of the traditional
App Engine standard sandbox

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:37.530
restrictions.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:41.880
You can run basically whatever
framework that you want to run.

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.610
But Rajeev, do you
want to add to that?

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:45.450
RAJEEV DAYAL: Yeah.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.190
So I think you covered it well.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.260
I guess what I would add is--

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:53.430
there is a bunch of work
that is in progress--

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.320
so it isn't there yet--

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.040
to integrate more of these
frameworks in with GCP.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.440
So for example,
in Spring, there's

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.510
things like Spring Cloud Stream.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:07.960
And we want to integrate that
in with our Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:09.710
so that you have a
nice integration there.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:11.940
You could wire it up
yourself, if you wanted to.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.770
But that's obviously
a bunch of work.

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:16.470
And we want to have something
like that ready for you when

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:17.700
you come to the platform.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:21.570
Another example is-- so we have
the Stackdriver diagnostics

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:22.980
platform.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.590
That's part of GCP.

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.410
So you know this gives you
information on your application

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:29.910
as it runs.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:30.750
You have stats.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:33.910
You see like traces and whatnot.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.390
And you see like logging,
error reporting--

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:40.230
we want to wire that into what
Spring also has in that vein,

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:42.160
like Spring Actuator
and whatnot, as well.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.642
So basically you can
get that for free,

00:20:45.642 --> 00:20:47.850
as opposed to having to wire
all of that up yourself.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.820
So these are integrations
that we're currently

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:51.720
working on right now.

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:52.614
They don't exist yet.

00:20:52.614 --> 00:20:54.780
But we want to make it a
much better experience when

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.321
you come to our
platform with Spring,

00:20:56.321 --> 00:20:57.900
and you just get a
lot more for free.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:58.650
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:00.400
Since we're talking about
