00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:05.774
be excited about?

00:21:05.774 --> 00:21:07.690
RAY TSANG: So one thing
that I want to mention

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:10.440
is something that actually
just came out, I think,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:11.760
before the new year.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:13.900
It is our ZipKin connectors.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:17.130
So if anyone is interested
in developing microservices

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:22.470
in general, you might need
to be able to trace the calls

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.090
across multiple services.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:27.990
And in the Java world, a
lot of it is using ZipKin,

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:31.260
and you can tie it up with
Spring Boot if you want to.

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:34.460
So rather than maintaining,
say, your own ZipKin server,

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:36.420
there is actually
a proxy, now, that

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:41.070
can take in ZipKin
requests for the tracing,

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:44.430
and then it can forward the
trace information directly

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:47.430
into Cloud Stackdriver Trace.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.432
So what that means is you
have less to maintain,

00:21:49.432 --> 00:21:51.390
and you don't have to
change your code in order

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:53.330
to be able to trace
your applications.

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:56.250
You can browse all the
trace information directly

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:57.960
from the GCP console.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:21:59.460
RAJEEV DAYAL:
Another cool thing--

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:02.190
it isn't super new, but I
don't know how well known

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:04.260
it is-- is the Cloud
Debugger, actually.

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:06.900
So this is a pretty cool
piece of technology.

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.300
It's actually what
you might call

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.150
like a post-mortem debugger,
or it's a production debugger.

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:16.260
So when you set a breakpoint,
you're not actually

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:17.700
stopping execution.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:20.390
What you're actually doing
is you're saying that,

00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:23.880
OK, you want to take a sample or
trace of some of the requests--

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.370
sort of a snapshot of the
stack at that point in time--

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:28.260
so that you can come back later.

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:30.610
And you can sort
of view-- like, OK,

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:32.610
when I hit this when I
hit this breakpoint, what

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:33.855
was going on at this time?

00:22:33.855 --> 00:22:35.730
And this is pretty cool,
because normally you

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.624
can't use this type of feature
in production-- because you

00:22:38.624 --> 00:22:41.040
would halt execution, and then
no one can go to your site.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.460
And that's no good, right?

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:45.330
But what we've done
here is now you can even

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:46.930
set conditional breakpoints.

00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:48.989
So like, on a certain
condition you trip this.

00:22:48.989 --> 00:22:51.030
And then you can be like,
oh, what happened here?

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:51.870
This was hit.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:52.530
Why was it hit?

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:53.850
Let me walk through.

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.550
And we've actually wired
integration into IntelliJ.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.590
So you can actually
walk through this stack

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:00.660
and look at the
stuff as you would

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.971
just in a normal debugger.

00:23:01.971 --> 00:23:03.720
So you're not actually
breaking execution,

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.090
but at least you have a
familiar interface into that.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.000
And they've recently
added a new feature--

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.760
I think it's in the web UI, we
haven't added it the IDE yet--

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.860
which is called like
dynamic log points.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:16.050
So how many times
you had like, where

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:18.592
you want to know
more information,

00:23:18.592 --> 00:23:20.550
because something is
going wrong in production,

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:22.350
and you're like,
oh, jeez, I wish

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:23.820
I had added that log statement.

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:25.611
And then you go, you
add the log statement.

00:23:25.611 --> 00:23:26.370
You rebuild.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.050
Whatever-- you redeploy.

00:23:28.050 --> 00:23:29.065
Hours go by.

00:23:29.065 --> 00:23:31.190
And then you see the log,
and you're like, oh, wow.

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:32.510
I wish I had even more log info.

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:33.926
And then you do
that again, right?

00:23:33.926 --> 00:23:37.040
With dynamic logs,
you actually can set--

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:40.100
at a point in the code that's in
production-- on a source line,

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.040
and say like, I want to
print this information--

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:44.090
these variables.

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:46.460
And it actually sets
that dynamically--

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:47.930
like in the bytecode.

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:49.820
So without having to
redeploy or anything,

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:52.670
you actually can set a
logging statement in there,

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.860
and you'll start seeing
the logging statements

00:23:54.860 --> 00:23:55.610
as they're hit.

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:59.690
So that's like a really powerful
feature to sort figure out

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:02.630
what's going on production,
saving you probably

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:05.660
like hours or days
of redeploying

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:08.310
with more logs and more
logs and more logs.

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:09.644
So that's a really cool feature.

00:24:09.644 --> 00:24:10.559
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

